1
00:00:09,690 --> 00:00:15,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I wanted to ask about mindfulness of breathing, and how that should be done.

2
00:00:17,855 --> 00:00:24,199
[SPEAKER_01]: We can read the Sutta over and over again, and try and follow some sort of method, step by step.

3
00:00:24,919 --> 00:00:26,460
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll breathe in, then I'll breathe out.

4
00:00:28,021 --> 00:00:29,602
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll breathe in, thinking about my body.

5
00:00:29,722 --> 00:00:32,184
[SPEAKER_01]: I attend to this, I attend to that, I do this, I do that.

6
00:00:34,045 --> 00:00:39,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can go, you can actually run through all those steps, forwards, backwards, whatever you want.

7
00:00:40,912 --> 00:00:42,613
[SPEAKER_00]: And still not do the mindfulness of breathing.

8
00:00:44,074 --> 00:00:44,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

9
00:00:44,274 --> 00:00:45,675
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what are you supposed to do?

10
00:00:52,699 --> 00:00:55,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, as the name itself says, it's mindfulness.

11
00:00:56,281 --> 00:00:57,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of breathing.

12
00:00:57,902 --> 00:01:00,884
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to do mindfulness of breathing, you need to know what mindfulness is.

13
00:01:03,206 --> 00:01:03,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

14
00:01:03,486 --> 00:01:04,406
[SPEAKER_01]: What is mindfulness?

15
00:01:04,506 --> 00:01:05,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

16
00:01:05,827 --> 00:01:10,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness, as we spoke in other talks,

17
00:01:11,497 --> 00:01:13,698
[SPEAKER_00]: If mindfulness is done rightly, it's effortless.

18
00:01:13,898 --> 00:01:15,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness is not something you can do.

19
00:01:16,899 --> 00:01:25,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is something you discern, on account of the presently enduring things, experiences as a whole, feelings, body, any...

20
00:01:25,703 --> 00:01:31,165
[SPEAKER_00]: There are many different ways you can establish mindfulness, but principle of mindfulness is the same, to be discerned.

21
00:01:32,566 --> 00:01:33,946
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is it that you discern?

22
00:01:36,703 --> 00:01:41,567
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to become mindful, well, you, for example, you discern your presently enduring situation.

23
00:01:43,508 --> 00:01:44,189
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have an option.

24
00:01:44,209 --> 00:01:47,091
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be absorbed with the presently enduring situation.

25
00:01:47,171 --> 00:01:51,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Something can take away 100% of your attention.

26
00:01:51,914 --> 00:01:55,417
[SPEAKER_00]: You're dealing with this problem or looking at this or doing that.

27
00:01:56,278 --> 00:02:01,922
[SPEAKER_00]: But including self-awareness into whatever you're doing, that's already like a step closer to the proper mindfulness.

28
00:02:03,177 --> 00:02:04,117
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not something you can do.

29
00:02:04,157 --> 00:02:07,439
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like an attitude that you can have in regard to things you do.

30
00:02:08,419 --> 00:02:13,721
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, fundamentally, a meditation method is inherently wrong from the point of view of mindfulness.

31
00:02:14,001 --> 00:02:22,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't be doing the mindfulness, yet, as the Buddha said, meditation is nothing other than unshakable, imperturbable establishment of mindfulness.

32
00:02:23,926 --> 00:02:29,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're not doing mindfulness from the start, how can you magically establish it through something you do, some methods or repetitions?

33
00:02:31,329 --> 00:02:32,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a recognition

34
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:45,931
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, I'm sitting on the chair now, and with the corner of your eye, with the back of your mind, you're aware of that.

35
00:02:47,092 --> 00:02:54,458
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean I need to stand up, or lay down, or start walking, in order to know that I was seated on the chair.

36
00:02:55,149 --> 00:02:58,590
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the fundamental, like the bodily postures or just awareness of the body.

37
00:02:59,150 --> 00:03:13,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness done properly means basically awareness of your situation, be it... whether it's kind of established upon the body or feelings or the states of mind, the moods, means it's established in that background.

38
00:03:13,193 --> 00:03:14,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not attending it.

39
00:03:14,114 --> 00:03:14,374
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not...

40
00:03:14,394 --> 00:03:18,034
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to keep sitting here, I'm seated, I'm seated, I'm seated.

41
00:03:18,114 --> 00:03:19,775
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to keep repeating that to myself.

42
00:03:19,815 --> 00:03:19,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

43
00:03:20,015 --> 00:03:24,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I already know that I'm seated, even if I don't think about it.

44
00:03:25,085 --> 00:03:27,467
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to ask me, what's your body posture?

45
00:03:27,507 --> 00:03:28,548
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll immediately say, I'm seated.

46
00:03:29,708 --> 00:03:32,511
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you want to tap into, so to speak.

47
00:03:32,531 --> 00:03:35,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Proper development of mindfulness is tapping into what you already know.

48
00:03:36,333 --> 00:03:40,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not doing what you think you must be doing.

49
00:03:40,056 --> 00:03:45,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm seated, I'm seated, I'm feeling the chair, I'm feeling the chair, my back, my legs are folded, I'm feeling the floor.

50
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:48,643
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all result of you already knowing that you are seated.

51
00:03:49,063 --> 00:03:50,524
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not your sitting.

52
00:03:51,104 --> 00:03:54,547
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, whether you're attending to your feet and your back and whatever else going on,

53
00:03:55,126 --> 00:04:00,370
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're seated, that's secondary to the fact that you're already seated, and you already know that.

54
00:04:01,430 --> 00:04:12,478
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of like giving in to that, oh, I'm doing this, I'm doing this, almost like basically exercising your sense of control over your meditation technique, you want to recognize that, wait a minute, I already know what I'm doing.

55
00:04:12,938 --> 00:04:16,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And then just try not to forget that little bit, that one percent, that one grain.

56
00:04:16,981 --> 00:04:18,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't forget what you already know.

57
00:04:19,383 --> 00:04:24,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you don't need active effort of reminding yourself that you're seated, that you're seated, that you're seated.

58
00:04:25,096 --> 00:04:30,458
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is just keep a corner of your eye on the fact that you already know that you're seated.

59
00:04:30,578 --> 00:04:31,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And how do you know?

60
00:04:31,458 --> 00:04:32,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you are seated.

61
00:04:34,119 --> 00:04:35,620
[SPEAKER_00]: So that knowledge doesn't come from you.

62
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:37,721
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a recognition of how things are.

63
00:04:38,841 --> 00:04:42,662
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why through that mindfulness you learn how to discern how things are.

64
00:04:43,222 --> 00:04:50,205
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that type of mindfulness will result in discerning impermanence, suffering, lack of control, all of that.

65
00:04:50,692 --> 00:04:58,035
[SPEAKER_00]: But it won't result if you do it on your own terms, which is, this is what I'm doing, this is my meditation, I'm touching, touching, feeling, feeling, sitting, sitting.

66
00:05:00,916 --> 00:05:03,457
[SPEAKER_00]: That's you basically doing what you think you should be doing.

67
00:05:03,497 --> 00:05:09,319
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact is, Putujan already starts with the wrong view, which means whatever he thinks he should be doing, he will be doing it with the wrong view.

68
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:15,141
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can't have too much mindfulness either, because it's not doing.

69
00:05:15,161 --> 00:05:18,142
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the recognition of what you already know, and that knowledge is not on you.

70
00:05:19,323 --> 00:05:19,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

71
00:05:20,581 --> 00:05:23,803
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to body postures or in regard to the presently enduring feeling.

72
00:05:24,843 --> 00:05:27,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, that's something you already know.

73
00:05:27,164 --> 00:05:28,625
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I ask you, how do you feel?

74
00:05:29,986 --> 00:05:34,748
[SPEAKER_00]: You might think about how you feel in this particular regard or that particular, but if I ask you, how do you feel overall?

75
00:05:34,968 --> 00:05:35,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it okay?

76
00:05:35,948 --> 00:05:37,149
[SPEAKER_00]: How's your mood, generally?

77
00:05:37,609 --> 00:05:42,211
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll immediately know whether it's sort of plus or minus, so to speak.

78
00:05:42,251 --> 00:05:44,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's okay, whether it isn't okay.

79
00:05:45,073 --> 00:05:45,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's neither.

80
00:05:46,869 --> 00:05:51,572
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's tapping into what you already know, and you already know it because it's present on its own terms.

81
00:05:51,833 --> 00:05:54,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is there enduring, whichever it is.

82
00:05:55,075 --> 00:06:07,784
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's because of you not keeping a corner of your eye on what you already know, that's why you get sort of, eh... distracted, pulled with sensuality, ill-will, reacting, causing more trouble for yourself, all these things.

83
00:06:09,966 --> 00:06:15,970
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people are afraid to be alone as well, because they depend on something to take away their attention, to have something to attend to,

84
00:06:16,488 --> 00:06:22,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Including the meditation technique, or this, or this, or this, and they don't stay with what they already know, because it's... it's frightening.

85
00:06:24,173 --> 00:06:25,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, at least.

86
00:06:25,493 --> 00:06:31,917
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to just not do anything, including your meditation technique, like, the pressure is going to start building up.

87
00:06:32,397 --> 00:06:33,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Solitude, not doing anything.

88
00:06:34,459 --> 00:06:36,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, doing is required for the maintenance of your being.

89
00:06:38,961 --> 00:06:41,783
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why many people depend on activity of all sorts.

90
00:06:43,785 --> 00:06:48,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can have a good activity, bad activity, less bad activity, but overall, you depend on the activity.

91
00:06:50,429 --> 00:06:52,611
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness is not an activity.

92
00:06:53,772 --> 00:07:00,896
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can't have too much of it, and that's why you can actually develop wisdom on account of it, if you start discerning it rightly.

93
00:07:01,277 --> 00:07:05,199
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you need to know what mindfulness is, in order to do it rightly.

94
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:09,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just, okay, I'm mindful now.

95
00:07:09,402 --> 00:07:13,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can't start perfectly, you can't start rightly from the beginning,

96
00:07:15,105 --> 00:07:20,368
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can certainly arrive at it if you don't take for granted what you think mindfulness is as mindfulness.

97
00:07:21,189 --> 00:07:24,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if I were to know what mindfulness is, I would have at least the right to view.

98
00:07:25,911 --> 00:07:27,172
[SPEAKER_00]: I would be at least a sotāpanna.

99
00:07:27,993 --> 00:07:30,574
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I'm not sotāpanna, it means I don't quite know what mindfulness is.

100
00:07:31,075 --> 00:07:33,536
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever I think mindfulness is, I must upgrade.

101
00:07:34,297 --> 00:07:37,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that, I must discern it further.

102
00:07:37,178 --> 00:07:38,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, it's a win-win.

103
00:07:38,699 --> 00:07:40,420
[SPEAKER_00]: If you already know what mindfulness is, great.

104
00:07:41,101 --> 00:07:43,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing more of it cannot bring any harm.

105
00:07:44,028 --> 00:07:48,891
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know what mindfulness is and then you end up doing more and you understand what mindfulness is, you win again.

106
00:07:48,911 --> 00:07:54,114
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have nothing to lose by regarding yourself as not knowing quite what mindfulness is.

107
00:07:54,855 --> 00:07:56,436
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, pushing further.

108
00:07:56,776 --> 00:08:03,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the only thing you have to lose is your vanity and conceit that made you believe you already understood and know and it's already helpful and it already works.

109
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:08,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it does, you wouldn't have a problem upgrading it or pushing it further.

110
00:08:09,283 --> 00:08:11,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it truly works, it will stand that test.

111
00:08:12,865 --> 00:08:13,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing will happen to it.

112
00:08:14,050 --> 00:08:24,233
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that some people might be defending their meditation technique or something and get very touchy over it, or over their practice of mindfulness, means there's... basically, there's passion invested in it, literally.

113
00:08:25,374 --> 00:08:27,454
[SPEAKER_00]: There's conceit and vanity invested in it.

114
00:08:27,655 --> 00:08:28,755
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not mindfulness then.

115
00:08:32,136 --> 00:08:35,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now that we clarify what mindfulness is... Mindfulness of breathing.

116
00:08:37,258 --> 00:08:42,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you... you can do the breathing, but you can't do the mindfulness in mindfulness of breathing.

117
00:08:44,630 --> 00:08:50,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, saying mindfulness of breathing means knowledge of breathing, knowledge of the act of breathing.

118
00:08:51,934 --> 00:08:55,556
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the Buddha says in that sutra, knowingly he breathes in, knowingly he breathes out.

119
00:08:55,656 --> 00:08:56,816
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what his mindfulness is.

120
00:08:58,277 --> 00:09:00,798
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on that level of that discernment of what's already there.

121
00:09:01,418 --> 00:09:08,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you can be mindful of the body posture or you want to be mindful of something less static.

122
00:09:08,702 --> 00:09:09,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it that way.

123
00:09:10,663 --> 00:09:14,385
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be mindful of an activity, of something you do.

124
00:09:15,832 --> 00:09:20,116
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the neutral activity that your body does, whether you want it or not?

125
00:09:20,136 --> 00:09:21,617
[SPEAKER_00]: It's breathing in, breathing out.

126
00:09:24,340 --> 00:09:29,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to be aware of the background of your act of breathing in and breathing out.

127
00:09:29,885 --> 00:09:33,088
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you don't want to be attending to your breath directly.

128
00:09:33,669 --> 00:09:37,713
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to make it sort of become a background of whatever your mind is thinking.

129
00:09:39,014 --> 00:09:40,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're mindful of breathing.

130
00:09:41,910 --> 00:09:44,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can even bring it to the foreground if you want.

131
00:09:44,851 --> 00:09:48,672
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, not in a sense of, I'm perceiving my breath, I'm being nostrils, nostrils.

132
00:09:48,692 --> 00:09:48,812
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

133
00:09:48,932 --> 00:09:50,712
[SPEAKER_00]: It still has to be on the level of that knowledge.

134
00:09:51,492 --> 00:09:53,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I ask you now, are you breathing?

135
00:09:54,073 --> 00:09:55,073
[SPEAKER_00]: You will know that you are.

136
00:09:55,513 --> 00:10:00,095
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't have to stop breathing in order to give me the answer.

137
00:10:00,115 --> 00:10:01,175
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you need to wait a minute.

138
00:10:02,155 --> 00:10:03,035
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even doubt it.

139
00:10:03,936 --> 00:10:05,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have to stop breathing to give me the answer.

140
00:10:06,396 --> 00:10:08,217
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even doubt whether you're breathing or not.

141
00:10:08,517 --> 00:10:09,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you already know that you're breathing.

142
00:10:11,472 --> 00:10:13,773
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you want to learn through the mindfulness of breathing.

143
00:10:14,414 --> 00:10:15,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognition of what you already know.

144
00:10:16,435 --> 00:10:21,718
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not something you always have to maintain, or even can maintain, a hundred percent, if I'm breathing, I'm breathing.

145
00:10:21,738 --> 00:10:21,918
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

146
00:10:22,818 --> 00:10:25,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Let it, let it drift away, without losing the sight of it.

147
00:10:27,721 --> 00:10:39,047
[SPEAKER_00]: So, here, here I am, sitting, semi-comfortably, not too comfortably, because you'll fall asleep, wide awake, eyes open, and you're breathing.

148
00:10:41,807 --> 00:10:44,229
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you know that you know that you are breathing.

149
00:10:45,449 --> 00:10:45,830
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

150
00:10:47,110 --> 00:10:47,891
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do next?

151
00:10:48,071 --> 00:10:48,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

152
00:10:48,471 --> 00:10:48,992
[SPEAKER_00]: You do that.

153
00:10:51,233 --> 00:10:55,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is the in-breath, there is the out-breath, there is the in-breath, there is the out-breath.

154
00:10:56,256 --> 00:10:59,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, they all stand within that knowledge of I am breathing.

155
00:11:00,879 --> 00:11:04,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's a particular in-breath or particular out-breath, the knowledge of breathing remains the same.

156
00:11:05,418 --> 00:11:13,339
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why then, the Buddha would say, he would breathe in like this, or like this, or short breath, or long breath, or like this breath, or like that breath.

157
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:17,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Point being, the knowledge of that breathing still stays the same.

158
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:24,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness gets established, regardless of the type of breath, or how different it is.

159
00:11:24,742 --> 00:11:25,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the same principle.

160
00:11:25,902 --> 00:11:26,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is the same principle.

161
00:11:27,242 --> 00:11:28,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the mindful of the body.

162
00:11:29,322 --> 00:11:32,103
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Buddha would say he's mindful of the body, like this, mindful of standing, or

163
00:11:33,077 --> 00:11:37,960
[SPEAKER_00]: He's mindful to the extent necessary, for final knowledge, that body is there.

164
00:11:39,661 --> 00:11:51,908
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, the ultimate background of any of your activity is the background of your body being present there as a basis on account of which you can do all these activities and engagements, feel feelings, perceive perceptions and so on.

165
00:11:52,468 --> 00:11:59,472
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your mind never forgets that 0.01% what it already knows of that body being a necessary basis for it,

166
00:12:01,187 --> 00:12:03,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind cannot give rise to a vijja.

167
00:12:03,630 --> 00:12:05,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you recognize it at that time or not, doesn't matter.

168
00:12:06,653 --> 00:12:07,654
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it rightly.

169
00:12:07,694 --> 00:12:13,340
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're doing it rightly, the time will come when you've done it rightly sufficiently, that you recognize what you've been doing rightly.

170
00:12:14,762 --> 00:12:18,546
[SPEAKER_00]: That's also another reason why nobody can accidentally stumble upon the right view.

171
00:12:19,273 --> 00:12:26,134
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes repetition, repetition, repetition of something you took either through, like, intellectual discernment or on faith, but you've been doing it rightly.

172
00:12:26,594 --> 00:12:31,976
[SPEAKER_00]: You do it rightly sufficiently, then you recognize right as right, which is the basic definition of Sotāpanna.

173
00:12:32,516 --> 00:12:35,636
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows kusala as kusala and kusala as a kusala.

174
00:12:36,516 --> 00:12:44,398
[SPEAKER_00]: But he wouldn't have arrived at it if he haven't been doing it rightly on account of utterance of another and his own faith in it and rightly understanding it on that intellectual level.

175
00:12:46,250 --> 00:12:48,951
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness of breathing, same principle of mindfulness.

176
00:12:49,571 --> 00:12:50,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing is there.

177
00:12:50,411 --> 00:12:54,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's a short breath, long breath, quick breath, slow breath, breathing is there.

178
00:12:54,933 --> 00:12:55,973
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you know.

179
00:12:57,854 --> 00:13:02,236
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a way, breathing is doing, but knowledge of the breathing is not the doing.

180
00:13:03,176 --> 00:13:04,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would the Buddha then say doing?

181
00:13:04,616 --> 00:13:06,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, sometimes you want to do something.

182
00:13:06,717 --> 00:13:09,158
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to replace coarse doing with refined doing.

183
00:13:09,598 --> 00:13:12,039
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to replace unwholesome doing with wholesome doing.

184
00:13:13,590 --> 00:13:20,015
[SPEAKER_00]: And breathing being the most neutral type of doing, is the wholesomeness doing you can do.

185
00:13:20,035 --> 00:13:20,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's just neutral.

186
00:13:22,156 --> 00:13:24,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't harm anyone, doesn't harm you, doesn't harm others.

187
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:31,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can develop mindfulness, you can develop knowledge of that neutral type of doing.

188
00:13:33,264 --> 00:13:40,830
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's also repetitive doing, which means it doesn't require your attention as if like solving a task or doing some like menial work or whatever.

189
00:13:41,470 --> 00:13:41,590
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

190
00:13:41,771 --> 00:13:42,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just breathing.

191
00:13:42,737 --> 00:13:44,498
[SPEAKER_00]: It happens whether you think about it or not.

192
00:13:46,418 --> 00:13:50,619
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to discern it on the level of doing, but discern it, not do it yourself.

193
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:56,041
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you do that?

194
00:13:56,061 --> 00:13:59,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Start breathing, secluded, alone.

195
00:14:01,882 --> 00:14:03,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask yourself, am I breathing right now?

196
00:14:04,643 --> 00:14:04,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

197
00:14:06,704 --> 00:14:10,325
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already like an increment of that right direction of that knowledge.

198
00:14:10,385 --> 00:14:11,525
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you already know you're breathing.

199
00:14:17,473 --> 00:14:19,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you still breathing?

200
00:14:19,575 --> 00:14:19,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

201
00:14:21,116 --> 00:14:22,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you still know you're breathing.

202
00:14:22,437 --> 00:14:23,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Still the same breathing.

203
00:14:23,237 --> 00:14:23,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Same knowledge.

204
00:14:24,398 --> 00:14:24,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

205
00:14:25,479 --> 00:14:26,880
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have same thing enduring now.

206
00:14:29,582 --> 00:14:32,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you don't even have to go as to ask yourself whether you're breathing.

207
00:14:35,066 --> 00:14:36,647
[SPEAKER_00]: You just remind yourself what you already know.

208
00:14:36,687 --> 00:14:39,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you are still breathing.

209
00:14:39,449 --> 00:14:41,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can stop saying still.

210
00:14:41,711 --> 00:14:42,912
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just say, yeah, I'm breathing.

211
00:14:43,652 --> 00:14:44,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't even have to say it.

212
00:14:44,353 --> 00:14:44,893
[SPEAKER_00]: You can think it.

213
00:14:46,292 --> 00:14:52,993
[SPEAKER_00]: When you become familiar with that, as in right now, I'm still breathing, you can then look within that.

214
00:14:55,294 --> 00:14:56,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the breath is quite slow right now.

215
00:14:59,155 --> 00:14:59,995
[SPEAKER_00]: And you already know that.

216
00:15:03,156 --> 00:15:05,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not doing it, you're discerning it, what's being done.

217
00:15:08,417 --> 00:15:14,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can do it, but you realize that you yourself doing it, is secondary to discernment of what's being done.

218
00:15:15,773 --> 00:15:18,996
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's incidentally how you also overcome the nature of action, how you disown it.

219
00:15:19,656 --> 00:15:20,997
[SPEAKER_00]: But let's not complicate things here.

220
00:15:22,519 --> 00:15:25,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you still breathing?

221
00:15:25,381 --> 00:15:25,701
[SPEAKER_00]: You are.

222
00:15:27,143 --> 00:15:29,865
[SPEAKER_00]: All this time you had the same mindfulness of the same breathing.

223
00:15:31,046 --> 00:15:34,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, particularly the breath kept going in, kept going out.

224
00:15:34,609 --> 00:15:35,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Overall, breathing is there.

225
00:15:37,251 --> 00:15:40,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Body sits, body stands up, body goes, body lays down.

226
00:15:40,814 --> 00:15:41,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Body is there.

227
00:15:43,417 --> 00:15:48,480
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why ānāpānasati results in the four foundations of mindfulness being brought to fulfillment, as the Buddha said.

228
00:15:49,341 --> 00:15:53,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't really result in mystical experiences of... of meditative lights and what not.

229
00:15:54,284 --> 00:15:55,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Results in clarity of mindfulness.

230
00:16:00,008 --> 00:16:06,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity of mindfulness results in the four right strivings, four right strivings in noble... in the enlightenment factors, and that's it.

231
00:16:06,773 --> 00:16:08,074
[SPEAKER_00]: No other work for you to be done.

232
00:16:08,794 --> 00:16:08,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

233
00:16:08,954 --> 00:16:13,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you brought that knowledge of the nature of things to fulfillment.

234
00:16:16,661 --> 00:16:17,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

235
00:16:18,762 --> 00:16:22,804
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, bringing mindfulness of breathing to the fore means, okay, am I breathing now?

236
00:16:24,304 --> 00:16:25,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

237
00:16:25,985 --> 00:16:26,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

238
00:16:28,826 --> 00:16:30,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I still aware that I am breathing now?

239
00:16:31,207 --> 00:16:31,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

240
00:16:34,208 --> 00:16:39,350
[SPEAKER_00]: How am I breathing now?

241
00:16:39,390 --> 00:16:40,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Medium pace, you can call it.

242
00:16:40,811 --> 00:16:41,731
[SPEAKER_00]: You can designate it.

243
00:16:41,771 --> 00:16:42,872
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no right answer to it.

244
00:16:43,732 --> 00:16:45,613
[SPEAKER_00]: There's only right direction or wrong direction.

245
00:16:45,912 --> 00:16:47,913
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, there's only knowledge of it or no knowledge of it.

246
00:16:48,714 --> 00:16:54,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, by the way, how am I feeling while I'm breathing right now?

247
00:16:54,457 --> 00:16:55,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Overall, fundamentally pleasant.

248
00:16:57,258 --> 00:16:58,139
[SPEAKER_00]: But am I still breathing?

249
00:16:58,159 --> 00:16:58,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

250
00:16:59,740 --> 00:17:07,904
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your mindfulness is still concerned, for the lack of a better word, anchored, for the lack of a better word, in the act of breathing.

251
00:17:09,311 --> 00:17:12,714
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the thoughts that keep popping up in my mind while I'm breathing?

252
00:17:12,934 --> 00:17:18,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Same breathing, same mindfulness, same knowledge of the same breathing.

253
00:17:18,939 --> 00:17:22,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there are thoughts maybe I'm thinking, how long can I do this?

254
00:17:22,663 --> 00:17:24,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Or what do I do next?

255
00:17:26,126 --> 00:17:29,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so are those thoughts pleasant or unpleasant while I'm breathing this?

256
00:17:29,469 --> 00:17:31,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I didn't stop breathing while I'm thinking all of this.

257
00:17:32,211 --> 00:17:35,554
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just I haven't lost the sight as the basis of all these things.

258
00:17:37,053 --> 00:17:40,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'm feeling maybe like doubtful or whatever people might feel.

259
00:17:41,076 --> 00:17:43,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you discover, as I said, there is no right answer.

260
00:17:43,357 --> 00:17:46,539
[SPEAKER_00]: There is only losing the sight of your breathing or not losing the sight of your breathing.

261
00:17:51,343 --> 00:17:53,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, whatever, mind stop thinking about it.

262
00:17:55,165 --> 00:17:57,307
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the state of my mind while I'm breathing this?

263
00:17:57,787 --> 00:17:58,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I depressed?

264
00:17:58,507 --> 00:17:59,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I elated?

265
00:18:00,168 --> 00:18:00,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I happy?

266
00:18:00,729 --> 00:18:01,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I sad?

267
00:18:01,469 --> 00:18:02,310
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my mood?

268
00:18:02,450 --> 00:18:05,412
[SPEAKER_00]: While I'm breathing in, breathing out, all this time,

269
00:18:09,811 --> 00:18:10,331
[SPEAKER_00]: And there you go.

270
00:18:11,431 --> 00:18:13,612
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to do anything else when it comes to mindfulness of breathing.

271
00:18:14,272 --> 00:18:14,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keep doing that.

272
00:18:16,153 --> 00:18:24,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you'll be developing mindfulness of body, mindfulness of feelings, mindfulness of the mind, and mindfulness of the thoughts, which is satipaṭṭhānas.

273
00:18:26,076 --> 00:18:27,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why ānāpānasati sutta says that.

274
00:18:28,976 --> 00:18:32,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Fulfillment of ānāpānasati brings the fulfillment of satipaṭṭhānas.

275
00:18:33,978 --> 00:18:34,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

276
00:18:38,951 --> 00:18:41,792
[SPEAKER_01]: It also says that you get rid of that distracting thought.

277
00:18:41,852 --> 00:18:42,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

278
00:18:42,433 --> 00:18:47,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're all rooted in losing the sight of that peripheral, in losing mindfulness, basically.

279
00:18:48,875 --> 00:18:52,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being anchored in the recognition of what's peripherally enduring.

280
00:18:52,797 --> 00:18:53,417
[SPEAKER_00]: The background.

281
00:18:53,698 --> 00:18:54,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

282
00:18:54,318 --> 00:18:55,458
[SPEAKER_00]: That's last aversion.

283
00:18:55,999 --> 00:18:59,360
[SPEAKER_00]: That is a necessary basis for last aversion and distraction to arise.

284
00:19:00,541 --> 00:19:02,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Losing the sight of what you already know.

285
00:19:03,442 --> 00:19:05,643
[SPEAKER_00]: That certain feelings, body,

286
00:19:06,221 --> 00:19:09,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Tensions, perceptions are enduring there on their own, in their background.

287
00:19:10,644 --> 00:19:12,405
[SPEAKER_00]: And you actively choose to ignore them.

288
00:19:14,326 --> 00:19:18,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, that's why everybody is fully responsible for the greed, aversion, delusion.

289
00:19:19,170 --> 00:19:20,671
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not, eh... accidental.

290
00:19:20,771 --> 00:19:25,414
[SPEAKER_00]: It's maintained through your consciously, basically, made decisions.

291
00:19:25,434 --> 00:19:28,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that you fully understand decisions you're making, but you are doing it.

292
00:19:28,796 --> 00:19:31,338
[SPEAKER_00]: You choose to go down the sensuality.

293
00:19:31,378 --> 00:19:34,960
[SPEAKER_00]: You choose to go down the... well, you choose to not say no.

294
00:19:35,364 --> 00:19:37,005
[SPEAKER_00]: To those things, including distraction.

295
00:19:37,025 --> 00:19:40,926
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the one who understands that, all he needs to do is stop delighting in it.

296
00:19:41,866 --> 00:19:46,027
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't stop thinking sensual thoughts or bad thoughts, but he stops delighting in them.

297
00:19:46,387 --> 00:19:47,428
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you stop thinking.

298
00:19:47,728 --> 00:19:51,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by preventing them from arising, but by not fueling them further.

299
00:19:56,030 --> 00:20:02,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And mindfulness through breathing is, eh... Like, see, if you're just mindful, I'm seated, I'm seated, I'm seated.

300
00:20:02,793 --> 00:20:03,873
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not doing anything.

301
00:20:04,942 --> 00:20:08,323
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could, you know, you could fall asleep or you could get easily distracted.

302
00:20:09,043 --> 00:20:13,324
[SPEAKER_00]: But breathing is something that's kind of done, which means it's more active.

303
00:20:14,945 --> 00:20:19,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, at the same time, the principle of mindfulness is not compromised, because it doesn't really take away your attention.

304
00:20:20,666 --> 00:20:26,368
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on the basis of knowing that breathing is there, you can discern what kind of breathing is there.

305
00:20:26,768 --> 00:20:30,729
[SPEAKER_00]: On the basis of knowing that breathing is there, you can discern what feelings are there.

306
00:20:31,382 --> 00:20:35,124
[SPEAKER_00]: On the basis of knowing the breathing is there, you can discern what mood is there.

307
00:20:36,364 --> 00:20:40,667
[SPEAKER_00]: On the basis of knowing what breathing is there, you can discern what exactly is your mind thinking right now.

308
00:20:40,707 --> 00:20:42,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Particular thoughts, or this thoughts, or that thoughts.

309
00:20:45,269 --> 00:20:48,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on the basis of doing things, you're discerning what's present.

310
00:20:50,011 --> 00:20:57,975
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop that, that means even less and less chances of being distracted, even when you're not practicing actively, whatever, any meditation.

311
00:20:58,754 --> 00:21:03,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're sweeping leaves, going, walking, sitting, extending, that mindfulness pertains to it.

312
00:21:05,837 --> 00:21:11,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, you don't necessarily have to think, I'm breathing, I'm breathing, I'm breathing, because you already know you're breathing.

313
00:21:12,240 --> 00:21:27,107
[SPEAKER_00]: You just learn, basically, how to tap into that point of view of that breathing being already there, of that body being already there, which automatically is something that is not your point of view, because you are not doing it.

314
00:21:36,324 --> 00:21:38,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it becomes before your point of view?

315
00:21:40,805 --> 00:21:44,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it becomes before your appropriation of the point of view through action.

316
00:21:46,806 --> 00:21:49,667
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I'm looking at this, I'm doing this, that's my point of view.

317
00:21:50,707 --> 00:21:55,588
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only way you can... the only reason you're able to do that is because these things are there on their own enduring already.

318
00:21:56,068 --> 00:21:58,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Bodies, their feelings and their perceptions are there.

319
00:21:58,729 --> 00:21:59,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentions are there, pretty much.

320
00:22:03,830 --> 00:22:04,210
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again,

321
00:22:05,122 --> 00:22:07,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, all this... there are no steps to mindfulness of breathing.

322
00:22:07,824 --> 00:22:12,908
[SPEAKER_00]: There is mindfulness of breathing and different aspects of it, of the same principle of mindfulness of breathing.

323
00:22:19,813 --> 00:22:20,594
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm breathing.

324
00:22:20,674 --> 00:22:21,795
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm still breathing.

325
00:22:21,815 --> 00:22:26,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Still the same mindfulness then, all this time, since we've been talking about it.

326
00:22:29,101 --> 00:22:31,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, lots of things have changed on a particular level.

327
00:22:32,443 --> 00:22:34,665
[SPEAKER_00]: New thoughts, new moods, new feelings, yet

328
00:22:35,029 --> 00:22:36,130
[SPEAKER_00]: That breathing is still the same.

329
00:22:37,591 --> 00:22:40,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness, knowledge of that same breathing is still there.

330
00:22:42,053 --> 00:22:46,236
[SPEAKER_01]: And that breathing is the nutriment, you could say?

331
00:22:46,336 --> 00:22:47,017
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a basis.

332
00:22:47,077 --> 00:22:48,297
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a reference point.

333
00:22:48,357 --> 00:22:48,918
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an anchor.

334
00:22:48,958 --> 00:22:51,459
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a background, basically, of everything else more particular.

335
00:22:57,523 --> 00:23:03,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, one could see that that's... eventually, at least, that that's... Well, there is no endgame.

336
00:23:03,087 --> 00:23:03,687
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no, like,

337
00:23:04,063 --> 00:23:05,204
[SPEAKER_00]: And then this happens.

338
00:23:06,005 --> 00:23:15,733
[SPEAKER_00]: If you just keep doing that, what's going to happen is, everything inferior to it, everything more particular to it, it's going to be purified of greed, aversion, delusion.

339
00:23:15,753 --> 00:23:19,876
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be purified of the basis of non-mindfulness.

340
00:23:19,996 --> 00:23:20,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

341
00:23:20,336 --> 00:23:22,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're developing basis of mindfulness.

342
00:23:23,199 --> 00:23:30,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're removing, through doing it, you're removing the nutriment for all the unwholesome things you've been doing and deciding to do.

343
00:23:34,145 --> 00:23:38,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of breathing is on the level of mindfulness of death, or any other mindfulness.

344
00:23:39,286 --> 00:23:42,206
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha taught it as a replacement to maraṇasati.

345
00:23:43,687 --> 00:23:48,147
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, okay, instead of that, because some monks couldn't quite handle it, practice this.

346
00:23:48,347 --> 00:24:00,510
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the same goal, as in stick, basically, with the most fundamental discernment of things not being in your control, being already there, enduring, despite whether you want them or not, whether you do them or not.

347
00:24:01,690 --> 00:24:03,330
[SPEAKER_00]: It is a death of your sense of self.

348
00:24:04,298 --> 00:24:05,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Death of your activity.

349
00:24:05,979 --> 00:24:07,199
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one who maintains them.

350
00:24:07,239 --> 00:24:08,320
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one who is in control.

351
00:24:09,601 --> 00:24:11,441
[SPEAKER_00]: That sense just gets completely pushed out.

352
00:24:11,521 --> 00:24:11,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Fades away.

353
00:24:14,163 --> 00:24:16,284
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you went into mindfulness of death.

354
00:24:19,505 --> 00:24:20,186
[SPEAKER_01]: How is that done?

355
00:24:20,326 --> 00:24:23,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is the death that you're mindful of?

356
00:24:23,407 --> 00:24:25,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, start with mindfulness of the body.

357
00:24:26,168 --> 00:24:33,652
[SPEAKER_00]: You start seeing the body as a necessary, organic, literally made out of organs,

358
00:24:36,425 --> 00:24:48,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Think, in the world there, because of which you experience the world and everything else in it, all your happiness and sadness and choices and decisions are inconceivable if the body is still not there, simultaneously present and living.

359
00:24:49,651 --> 00:24:50,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have any say in that?

360
00:24:51,832 --> 00:25:00,936
[SPEAKER_00]: That body that you find there, that you were given, certain height, certain complexion, whatever, can you make it not age?

361
00:25:01,516 --> 00:25:02,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you prevent it from getting sick?

362
00:25:03,836 --> 00:25:06,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you ultimately command to it to never die?

363
00:25:09,281 --> 00:25:12,043
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those are the first three basic things that people don't dwell on.

364
00:25:13,244 --> 00:25:18,389
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we say people are intoxicated with life, intoxicated with youth, intoxicated with health.

365
00:25:20,551 --> 00:25:26,776
[SPEAKER_00]: You... you choose to become intoxicated by refusing to look at the obvious characteristics of it, because they are unpleasant.

366
00:25:27,417 --> 00:25:28,638
[SPEAKER_00]: They fill you with anxiety.

367
00:25:37,150 --> 00:25:40,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's that inevitable possibility of death.

368
00:25:41,393 --> 00:25:41,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

369
00:25:41,634 --> 00:25:52,242
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you start recognizing that presence of the body, it's just a necessary nutriment for your experience as a whole of any kind, you stop being concerned with the particulars of that experience.

370
00:25:52,282 --> 00:25:53,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this bothers me.

371
00:25:53,063 --> 00:25:53,763
[SPEAKER_00]: This makes me happy.

372
00:25:53,783 --> 00:25:59,688
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize that its fundamental nature is undermined by the gratuitous presence of the living body.

373
00:26:01,730 --> 00:26:05,913
[SPEAKER_00]: And when that changes, none of it can remain standing a second longer.

374
00:26:06,324 --> 00:26:08,004
[SPEAKER_00]: None of your feelings or things can stay.

375
00:26:09,385 --> 00:26:13,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can they then be yours?

376
00:26:15,486 --> 00:26:28,910
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's basically beginning to acknowledge death, as in the distraction that is an impending upon the body, upon the senses, decay, fading away, disintegration.

377
00:26:30,199 --> 00:26:36,463
[SPEAKER_00]: You think about that, you're going to be less and less intoxicated with things that come through it, with your sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, thoughts.

378
00:26:37,704 --> 00:26:37,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

379
00:26:37,924 --> 00:26:46,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you recognize, well, whatever they are, they cannot step outside of the confinement of the basis, necessary basis of this living body.

380
00:26:48,692 --> 00:26:48,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

381
00:26:49,372 --> 00:26:49,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I breathing?

382
00:26:49,993 --> 00:26:50,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

383
00:26:50,693 --> 00:26:52,935
[SPEAKER_00]: While I'm breathing, what position is the body in?

384
00:26:55,277 --> 00:26:55,757
[SPEAKER_01]: It's seated.

385
00:26:56,237 --> 00:26:58,719
[SPEAKER_00]: That seated body, while I'm breathing in and out right now,

386
00:27:01,728 --> 00:27:02,989
[SPEAKER_00]: What are its characteristics?

387
00:27:03,890 --> 00:27:05,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Just describe it in any terms to yourself.

388
00:27:08,154 --> 00:27:10,336
[SPEAKER_00]: The most obvious one is that it's just there, isn't it?

389
00:27:11,296 --> 00:27:12,417
[SPEAKER_00]: It's living.

390
00:27:12,457 --> 00:27:13,138
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just there.

391
00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:17,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have say in any of those characteristics?

392
00:27:17,862 --> 00:27:18,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you change them?

393
00:27:18,563 --> 00:27:19,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you tell them to be otherwise?

394
00:27:20,865 --> 00:27:29,052
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while I'm breathing, and my body is seated while I'm breathing, that body that I'm discerning while I'm breathing,

395
00:27:29,638 --> 00:27:36,599
[SPEAKER_00]: In and out, while the breathing is there, that body that I'm discerning through it, it's independent of me, isn't it?

396
00:27:37,759 --> 00:27:41,260
[SPEAKER_00]: I can use it, but I cannot uphold it.

397
00:27:41,380 --> 00:27:43,080
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot step outside of it and own it.

398
00:27:43,300 --> 00:27:44,321
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot tell it what to do.

399
00:27:46,341 --> 00:27:50,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's only borrowed, which means everything else that you get through the body is equally borrowed.

400
00:27:51,302 --> 00:27:55,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And if something is borrowed, is it the right thing for me to regard it as mine and belonging to me?

401
00:27:56,023 --> 00:27:56,183
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

402
00:27:57,463 --> 00:27:58,443
[SPEAKER_00]: If something is borrowed,

403
00:27:58,900 --> 00:28:03,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the right thing for me to grieve and be sad over it being taken away?

404
00:28:03,662 --> 00:28:03,822
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

405
00:28:04,723 --> 00:28:11,726
[SPEAKER_00]: You require ignoring, active ignoring of the fact that you are not in control, in order to be... to grieve over it or be happy over it.

406
00:28:13,026 --> 00:28:17,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, mindfulness done rightly results in discernment of the natural principles, which is what dhamma is.

407
00:28:17,769 --> 00:28:18,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Nature.

408
00:28:19,569 --> 00:28:20,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Nature of things.

409
00:28:23,051 --> 00:28:27,613
[SPEAKER_00]: You could see how different that is from watching your nostrils, belly bowing or whatever they call it.

410
00:28:28,563 --> 00:28:29,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely different.

411
00:28:29,483 --> 00:28:30,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I'm doing this.

412
00:28:30,544 --> 00:28:34,485
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm feeling my belly rising and falling, rising and falling, rising and falling.

413
00:28:35,266 --> 00:28:36,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Zero discernment there.

414
00:28:36,466 --> 00:28:42,889
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just trying to attend the momentary presence of things, hoping that somehow, magically, it will result in knowledge.

415
00:28:45,150 --> 00:28:46,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Looking for meaning results in knowledge.

416
00:28:47,951 --> 00:28:54,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Delving with the knife, self-questioning, self-interrogation, all the things the Buddha talks about in the suttas, that's what results in the knowledge.

417
00:28:56,734 --> 00:28:58,215
[SPEAKER_01]: What about watching someone die?

418
00:29:01,041 --> 00:29:04,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, with the right attitude, sure, but not without the right attitude.

419
00:29:09,223 --> 00:29:23,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Watching someone die and then seeing that your body, your living body, right here, right now, is on the level, it's more related, it's more related to that dying body than to your sense of ownership of that body.

420
00:29:24,486 --> 00:29:28,667
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the same domain as the other dying bodies, not in the domain of you and this is my body.

421
00:29:30,123 --> 00:29:32,724
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you undermine your control, your sense of control.

422
00:29:33,084 --> 00:29:37,806
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't directly choose to not control it or not own it, but you can undermine it.

423
00:29:40,266 --> 00:29:50,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, seeing that body is impermanent, because of that body cannot be the reason for lasting satisfaction, automatically you experience that body is not mine.

424
00:29:52,570 --> 00:29:57,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Sābe sankhāra anicca, sābe sankhāra dukkha, sābe dhamma anatta.

425
00:30:24,255 --> 00:30:30,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in independent origination, it says, with birth, aging and death is.

426
00:30:31,877 --> 00:30:48,642
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with this life, this manifestation of life, and that's what I see as a whole, all that is determined by... or is subject to fading away, subject to ending.

427
00:30:49,403 --> 00:30:50,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yeah.

428
00:30:51,289 --> 00:30:55,110
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't need to be your subject or you don't need to be subjected to it.

429
00:30:55,970 --> 00:30:59,211
[SPEAKER_00]: You are subjected to it because you have appropriated.

430
00:30:59,411 --> 00:31:05,553
[SPEAKER_00]: You appropriated birth, you appropriated a given body, you appropriated possibilities of the given body and the senses.

431
00:31:06,513 --> 00:31:13,035
[SPEAKER_00]: You appropriated it by delighting in it, by pursuing pleasures that are secondary to it, that come on the basis of it.

432
00:31:14,355 --> 00:31:18,676
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't engage in sensual pleasure without appropriation of the body.

433
00:31:19,697 --> 00:31:20,677
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

434
00:31:22,646 --> 00:31:26,408
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through engaging in sensual pleasure, you're responsible for your appropriation of the body.

435
00:31:27,328 --> 00:31:30,809
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you are liable to whatever that body is liable to.

436
00:31:31,750 --> 00:31:34,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Accidents, aging, death, sickness, all of it.

437
00:31:36,252 --> 00:31:36,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, it's on you.

438
00:31:38,093 --> 00:31:41,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you took it through carelessly pursuing sensuality.

439
00:31:41,714 --> 00:31:43,915
[SPEAKER_01]: The body will always age and it will always die.

440
00:31:44,135 --> 00:31:44,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

441
00:31:44,835 --> 00:31:47,437
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stop regarding it as yours, it's not your problem.

442
00:31:47,917 --> 00:31:48,377
[SPEAKER_00]: In as much

443
00:31:48,971 --> 00:31:52,953
[SPEAKER_00]: These aging trees and other people's bodies are not your problem, because they're not yours.

444
00:31:53,093 --> 00:31:54,154
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they're not your problem.

445
00:31:55,034 --> 00:31:57,736
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that they're not your problem, because they're other people's.

446
00:31:58,836 --> 00:32:01,818
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not your problem, because other people you never considered to be yours.

447
00:32:02,618 --> 00:32:10,462
[SPEAKER_00]: But, incidentally, say if it's your son, your daughter, your partner, oh, suddenly you are emotionally affected.

448
00:32:10,502 --> 00:32:10,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

449
00:32:10,942 --> 00:32:12,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's mine.

450
00:32:12,923 --> 00:32:14,884
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you can appropriate other people as well.

451
00:32:15,425 --> 00:32:15,965
[SPEAKER_00]: My friend,

452
00:32:17,088 --> 00:32:18,169
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you suffer.

453
00:32:18,550 --> 00:32:19,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because they're dying.

454
00:32:20,112 --> 00:32:24,216
[SPEAKER_00]: You suffer because you appropriated them and now you're feeling that because you're liable to it.

455
00:32:25,838 --> 00:32:26,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have a choice.

456
00:32:27,520 --> 00:32:30,824
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to be free from suffering, you have to give up all the appropriation.

457
00:32:31,625 --> 00:32:36,350
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't want to give appropriation, that's absolutely fine, but then you make yourself liable to suffering.

458
00:32:37,149 --> 00:32:44,635
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, again, there's a contradiction, slight contradiction, I think, because if you were then to be honest with yourself and ask, why don't I want to give up all the appropriations?

459
00:32:44,815 --> 00:32:45,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's because it's unpleasant.

460
00:32:46,456 --> 00:32:49,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're still governed by the same principle of wanting to avoid suffering.

461
00:32:50,560 --> 00:32:54,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, that's why people protect their attachments and appropriations, because they don't want to suffer.

462
00:32:55,143 --> 00:32:57,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Ironically, you suffer because of that.

463
00:32:58,946 --> 00:33:03,450
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to take the initial hit of suffering, ultimately, you can free yourself from suffering.

464
00:33:04,531 --> 00:33:04,911
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you...

465
00:33:05,946 --> 00:33:11,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Too weak to take the initial hit of suffering, you make yourself liable to suffering indefinitely.

466
00:33:13,288 --> 00:33:16,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, everybody doesn't want to suffer.

467
00:33:17,270 --> 00:33:19,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Even people who say, I want to suffer, they don't.

468
00:33:21,071 --> 00:33:26,252
[SPEAKER_00]: They want to suffer because that provides them pleasure, because not getting that pleasure means suffering.

469
00:33:29,193 --> 00:33:34,515
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha himself could have not taught anything different than suffering and cessation of it.

470
00:33:34,973 --> 00:33:42,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is at the root of every... universally in every human, regardless of the culture, education, identity and what not.

471
00:33:43,676 --> 00:33:45,516
[SPEAKER_00]: That is always the basic principle.

472
00:33:48,117 --> 00:33:50,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Avoid pleasure, avoid pain, have pleasure.

473
00:33:52,178 --> 00:33:56,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having enough pleasure or getting more pleasure means avoiding pain of not having enough pleasure.

474
00:34:10,316 --> 00:34:17,080
[SPEAKER_01]: And what do you think about the common idea, or a lot of people can say, I'm not afraid of death.

475
00:34:17,820 --> 00:34:19,041
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not afraid of death.

476
00:34:20,762 --> 00:34:23,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, they're not afraid of what they think death is.

477
00:34:26,865 --> 00:34:28,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means they don't know what death is.

478
00:34:29,547 --> 00:34:33,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Because only, well, only two people are not afraid of death.

479
00:34:34,249 --> 00:34:38,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Either fully enlightened Arahant or fully deluded Pūtūjana.

480
00:34:40,738 --> 00:34:44,979
[SPEAKER_00]: But, one of them is fully liable to death, another one is fully free from it.

481
00:34:46,099 --> 00:34:48,000
[SPEAKER_00]: But they both are not afraid of it.

482
00:34:49,040 --> 00:34:56,122
[SPEAKER_00]: One is not afraid due to the sheer amount of ignorance and not even knowing what death is, another one is not afraid because he has completely overcome it.

483
00:34:58,722 --> 00:35:02,043
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you're an arahant, you are afraid of death.

484
00:35:02,763 --> 00:35:05,004
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't feel the fear, it's because you're covering it up.

485
00:35:07,244 --> 00:35:10,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't think that death is where you think death is,

486
00:35:12,892 --> 00:35:20,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Think that death is basically where you can't get your pleasures, where you can't get usage of your senses.

487
00:35:20,818 --> 00:35:25,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, find the fears you have in your life, and death is at the other end of them.

488
00:35:27,603 --> 00:35:32,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever those fears particularly might be, or whichever particular way they might have to manifest.

489
00:35:32,826 --> 00:35:36,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever brings people anxiety, death is on the other side of that.

490
00:35:36,969 --> 00:35:39,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're saying, oh, I'm not afraid of death, death brings me no anxiety.

491
00:35:39,631 --> 00:35:42,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because your idea of what death is, is completely wrong.

492
00:35:43,328 --> 00:35:48,150
[SPEAKER_00]: But something will be bringing you anxiety, and that's where the death is.

493
00:35:48,170 --> 00:35:50,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence the anxiety.

494
00:35:50,171 --> 00:35:53,092
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm afraid of losing all my friends.

495
00:35:53,552 --> 00:35:54,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Here you go.

496
00:35:55,993 --> 00:35:58,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Death is at the other end of that.

497
00:35:58,274 --> 00:35:59,755
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just a hint of where death is.

498
00:36:00,555 --> 00:36:01,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, not having anyone.

499
00:36:02,316 --> 00:36:02,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Being all alone.

500
00:36:03,336 --> 00:36:03,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Losing it all.

501
00:36:04,677 --> 00:36:07,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Or simply, losing things that are dear to me.

502
00:36:08,078 --> 00:36:12,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Or even more simply, as the Buddha said, being disjoined from the agreeable.

503
00:36:13,500 --> 00:36:18,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Being separated from the agreeable, being united with disagreeable, that's what suffering is.

504
00:36:19,344 --> 00:36:20,625
[SPEAKER_00]: And ultimate suffering is death.

505
00:36:22,387 --> 00:36:30,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'm not afraid of dying, but I'm afraid of what people think about me.

506
00:36:31,394 --> 00:36:31,814
[SPEAKER_00]: There you go.

507
00:36:32,955 --> 00:36:37,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Death is basically not having say in what people will think about you ultimately.

508
00:36:38,759 --> 00:36:40,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's the most frightening thing, isn't it?

509
00:36:41,181 --> 00:36:42,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, for those who fear that,

510
00:36:43,843 --> 00:36:46,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, I'm not afraid of dying, but I'm afraid of losing my wealth.

511
00:36:47,685 --> 00:36:49,386
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm afraid of losing my family.

512
00:36:49,426 --> 00:36:50,566
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm afraid of public speaking.

513
00:36:50,987 --> 00:36:52,207
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm afraid of public speaking.

514
00:36:52,247 --> 00:36:52,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

515
00:36:53,068 --> 00:36:58,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, literally, you are in... it's the ultimate experience of non-control.

516
00:36:59,151 --> 00:37:03,113
[SPEAKER_00]: You have control over your speaking, but you have zero control of how people will take that.

517
00:37:03,573 --> 00:37:04,233
[SPEAKER_00]: What will they say?

518
00:37:04,594 --> 00:37:05,374
[SPEAKER_00]: What will they think?

519
00:37:06,955 --> 00:37:08,916
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ultimately, what is then fear of death?

520
00:37:09,256 --> 00:37:11,157
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fear of the ultimate non-control.

521
00:37:11,797 --> 00:37:13,458
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not incidental that

522
00:37:14,030 --> 00:37:16,772
[SPEAKER_00]: One thing or the other will have to be reminding you of that.

523
00:37:17,312 --> 00:37:20,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the whole human experience is about covering the fact that you are not in control.

524
00:37:21,895 --> 00:37:23,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Covering the fact that you are liable to dying.

525
00:37:25,297 --> 00:37:27,778
[SPEAKER_00]: As in inevitably liable to dying.

526
00:37:33,982 --> 00:37:42,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all these things, loss and public speech, whatever frightens people, frightens them, because it's the ultimate reminder of the ultimate cessation of their

527
00:37:42,835 --> 00:37:44,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Any notion of control they might have.

528
00:37:45,196 --> 00:37:49,217
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when people say, I'm not afraid of dying, it's because that thought is in their control.

529
00:37:49,917 --> 00:37:51,457
[SPEAKER_00]: And they say, that's what dying is.

530
00:37:52,338 --> 00:37:53,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're not afraid of it.

531
00:37:53,878 --> 00:37:54,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody dies.

532
00:37:55,299 --> 00:37:55,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

533
00:37:57,479 --> 00:37:59,060
[SPEAKER_00]: But, why are you afraid of public speaking?

534
00:37:59,820 --> 00:38:00,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody does speaking.

535
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:01,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody speaks.

536
00:38:02,521 --> 00:38:04,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here you are terrified of public speaking.

537
00:38:06,082 --> 00:38:06,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

538
00:38:06,482 --> 00:38:10,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're constantly looking away from the fact that you are not in control.

539
00:38:12,621 --> 00:38:18,764
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you encounter experiences that you cannot ignore the fact that you're not in control, it frightens the hell out of you.

540
00:38:21,386 --> 00:38:22,386
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what death is.

541
00:38:23,287 --> 00:38:30,631
[SPEAKER_00]: The ultimate cessation of any ground for your notion of control that yet you take to be the most fundamental thing.

542
00:38:30,851 --> 00:38:33,853
[SPEAKER_00]: I am, therefore things are mine, therefore I'm in control.

543
00:38:35,454 --> 00:38:38,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anatta is basically seeing.

544
00:38:38,916 --> 00:38:41,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, anatta is seeing death, sure.

545
00:38:41,818 --> 00:38:50,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing death correctly means not being able to entertain the notion of control, means not being my own self, not being myself, not belonging to me, not appropriation.

546
00:38:50,485 --> 00:38:51,246
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all anatta.

547
00:38:52,667 --> 00:38:59,312
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I see that for myself, for myself, and then...

548
00:38:59,332 --> 00:39:00,453
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna scare the hell out of you.

549
00:39:00,793 --> 00:39:01,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

550
00:39:03,555 --> 00:39:04,856
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what happened in the suttas, didn't it?

551
00:39:05,937 --> 00:39:09,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if today people are not scared, when they read about anatta and

552
00:39:09,738 --> 00:39:12,761
[SPEAKER_00]: In nibbāna, it's because they don't have a clue what it is, what it really is.

553
00:39:13,641 --> 00:39:18,946
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for Putujana, nibbāna is closer to what death is, the one that he's ignoring.

554
00:39:18,986 --> 00:39:23,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Putujana, through ignoring death, actually ignores possibility of nibbāna.

555
00:39:25,131 --> 00:39:36,160
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why those people who came to the Buddha, the way the Buddha would lay it out on them, they couldn't ignore it and they would fall on the ground in complete anxious despair, have a mental breakdown, saying, this will kill me, this will destroy me.

556
00:39:37,165 --> 00:39:38,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because they still don't relinquish that.

557
00:39:39,146 --> 00:39:45,849
[SPEAKER_00]: They still gratuitously assume the priority of their sense of I am, despite the evidence showing the contrary.

558
00:39:47,550 --> 00:39:51,031
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but I'm not giving up I am, thus I will be destroyed.

559
00:39:53,952 --> 00:39:58,454
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you hear the teaching, you realize that could have not been yours even if you wanted it to.

560
00:40:01,416 --> 00:40:04,697
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only reason you're regarding it as yours is because you didn't know that it cannot be yours.

561
00:40:05,707 --> 00:40:07,167
[SPEAKER_00]: And how do you find out that it cannot be yours?

562
00:40:07,227 --> 00:40:14,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Through practicing mindfulness correctly and seeing it inseparable from the necessary basis of things that cannot be yours.

563
00:40:14,249 --> 00:40:25,332
[SPEAKER_00]: So, dwelling on the characteristics of the necessity of the body, dwelling on the characteristics of the necessity of breathing, dwelling on the independence of the present enduring feeling, present enduring perceptions, yet seeing yourself inseparable from it.

564
00:40:26,373 --> 00:40:35,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, basically, remolding, reshaping, going against the grain and forcing yourself to see that your own very self depends upon the basis that cannot be yourself.

565
00:40:35,923 --> 00:40:39,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it were so, the body would not age, would not get sick, it would not die.

566
00:40:40,286 --> 00:40:44,369
[SPEAKER_00]: If it were so, the feelings would not change, it would always be pleasant the way you wanted it.

567
00:40:45,470 --> 00:40:49,333
[SPEAKER_00]: But yet, the Buddha said, well, if that feeling is yours, which one is it?

568
00:40:50,093 --> 00:40:53,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you then experience displeasure, if feeling is yours?

569
00:40:54,276 --> 00:40:55,797
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't, if you're in control.

570
00:40:56,518 --> 00:41:01,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through reminding yourself that you're not in control, through practicing the maraṇasati correctly,

571
00:41:01,698 --> 00:41:04,639
[SPEAKER_00]: You get to undermine your sense of self gradually, gradually, gradually.

572
00:41:05,039 --> 00:41:07,100
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you won't freak out and have a breakdown.

573
00:41:07,240 --> 00:41:08,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, some monks did.

574
00:41:09,020 --> 00:41:12,202
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said, well, do mindfulness of breathing instead.

575
00:41:12,722 --> 00:41:16,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if done rightly, you result in the same principles being re... re... discerned.

576
00:41:19,524 --> 00:41:27,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if you are truly unafraid of death, how would you take other people's death?

577
00:41:28,987 --> 00:41:29,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Equally.

578
00:41:30,668 --> 00:41:31,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Equally unafraid of it.

579
00:41:32,657 --> 00:41:38,978
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be oblivious to what's happening, like some sort of robot who doesn't... who lost significance of things.

580
00:41:39,018 --> 00:41:39,178
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

581
00:41:39,759 --> 00:41:40,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Still your friend dying.

582
00:41:42,039 --> 00:41:51,621
[SPEAKER_00]: But, no amount of the extent of that experience, perception and feeling in regard to it, can sort of overturn your mind and make you think it's yours that which it isn't.

583
00:41:53,281 --> 00:41:54,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Or that it's permanent that which it isn't.

584
00:41:55,302 --> 00:42:00,203
[SPEAKER_00]: The only... the only attitude you could possibly have is, how could it be otherwise?

585
00:42:01,532 --> 00:42:05,955
[SPEAKER_00]: How can something, exactly, how can something that's been born and grown not die?

586
00:42:05,975 --> 00:42:11,338
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just like... Well, it's actually insanity to think that, to think otherwise.

587
00:42:12,539 --> 00:42:24,245
[SPEAKER_00]: So, grieving over death, it's on the level of insanity, because it's the most, like, it's inconceivable for somebody who understood the nature of death to think, yeah, but if only, it's just, it doesn't apply.

588
00:42:28,047 --> 00:42:29,188
[SPEAKER_00]: How can it be otherwise?

589
00:42:30,764 --> 00:42:32,264
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to get used to this, you know.

590
00:42:32,384 --> 00:42:36,346
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can't, you can't start doing this while you're...

591
00:42:36,946 --> 00:42:38,606
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it'd be difficult, while you're dying.

592
00:42:38,946 --> 00:42:40,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, good luck with that.

593
00:42:40,047 --> 00:42:47,649
[SPEAKER_00]: If you haven't done it while you were alive, if you haven't uncovered up the nature of death while you were alive, you're not gonna do it when you're dying.

594
00:42:47,949 --> 00:42:48,429
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too late.

595
00:42:49,949 --> 00:42:52,150
[SPEAKER_00]: So you want to start facing it as soon as you can.

596
00:43:02,260 --> 00:43:13,944
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, basically, understanding of death, understanding of the ultimate non-control, means ultimately giving things up, relinquishing appropriation, relinquishing assumption.

597
00:43:14,664 --> 00:43:17,005
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why understanding of death fully means nibbāna.

598
00:43:18,246 --> 00:43:27,649
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Sotāpanna, who understood the nature of nibbāna, or Ārya-sāvaka in general, often he said in sutta, oh, if he doesn't attain it during the life, he attains it when he's dying.

599
00:43:28,372 --> 00:43:28,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

600
00:43:28,612 --> 00:43:29,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where it comes from.

601
00:43:30,273 --> 00:43:32,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Comes from the direction of dying and cessation of control.

602
00:43:32,994 --> 00:43:33,795
[SPEAKER_00]: His understanding.

603
00:43:34,515 --> 00:43:36,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, he got careless and lazy during the lifetime.

604
00:43:36,776 --> 00:43:37,897
[SPEAKER_00]: He hasn't been making effort.

605
00:43:38,297 --> 00:43:41,238
[SPEAKER_00]: But death will force him in the direction that he already understood.

606
00:43:42,259 --> 00:43:46,861
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why sometimes somebody can become an arahant who had the right view at the moment of dying.

607
00:43:46,881 --> 00:43:49,362
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not some mystical light that appears before you die.

608
00:43:49,842 --> 00:43:53,144
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the direction you understood through your right view and everything else.

609
00:43:53,504 --> 00:43:54,404
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you're forced.

610
00:43:54,504 --> 00:43:55,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you're dragged there.

611
00:43:57,032 --> 00:44:01,695
[SPEAKER_00]: And you already understood it, so you basically you're dragged there where you should have gone while you were still alive.

612
00:44:01,915 --> 00:44:04,637
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still good to understand it.

613
00:44:07,158 --> 00:44:10,000
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's even better to do it and not wait to be dragged there.

614
00:44:11,801 --> 00:44:13,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Even for ārya-sāvakas.

615
00:44:20,926 --> 00:44:25,049
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you still breathing now?

616
00:44:25,069 --> 00:44:25,629
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm asking you.

617
00:44:25,649 --> 00:44:26,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you breathing now?

618
00:44:26,982 --> 00:44:27,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

619
00:44:30,923 --> 00:44:36,605
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is the experience of the breathing still the same experience of the breathing?

620
00:44:36,625 --> 00:44:38,505
[SPEAKER_01]: As far as I can tell, yeah, it's the same.

621
00:44:39,106 --> 00:44:41,086
[SPEAKER_00]: How can it be otherwise, unless you get another body?

622
00:44:47,868 --> 00:44:54,770
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while you're breathing right here and while we are talking while you're breathing, what is the general... what's your mood?

623
00:44:55,711 --> 00:44:56,731
[SPEAKER_00]: How are you feeling overall?

624
00:44:58,642 --> 00:44:59,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, overall, okay.

625
00:45:00,603 --> 00:45:01,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Could be better, I suppose.

626
00:45:02,284 --> 00:45:06,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Could be better while you're breathing in and out.

627
00:45:13,888 --> 00:45:19,471
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the pace of your breath that you discern being done while you're breathing?

628
00:45:21,231 --> 00:45:22,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, shallow.

629
00:45:22,552 --> 00:45:22,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Shallow.

630
00:45:22,952 --> 00:45:24,092
[SPEAKER_01]: A bit restricted.

631
00:45:24,192 --> 00:45:24,973
[SPEAKER_01]: A bit restricted.

632
00:45:26,894 --> 00:45:28,014
[SPEAKER_00]: All those things, basically,

633
00:45:29,200 --> 00:45:35,541
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just maintaining the mindfulness of breathing, keeping you sort of... all these questions.

634
00:45:37,382 --> 00:45:45,223
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you do it properly, long enough, you can stop asking questions, because you already know.

635
00:45:46,003 --> 00:45:52,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then we'll come down to... it's being breathed... it's being... the breathing in is being done shallowly.

636
00:45:53,405 --> 00:45:54,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Shallowly and breathing in.

637
00:45:54,932 --> 00:45:55,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Shallowly I'm breathing out.

638
00:45:56,133 --> 00:46:01,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, what's the experience of... what are the... what feelings am I experiencing while I'm breathing shallowly in and out?

639
00:46:02,516 --> 00:46:04,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you're not doing it of finding the answer.

640
00:46:04,777 --> 00:46:06,677
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just discerning what's already there.

641
00:46:06,757 --> 00:46:06,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

642
00:46:07,018 --> 00:46:08,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the feeling is already there.

643
00:46:08,698 --> 00:46:09,759
[SPEAKER_00]: The body is already there.

644
00:46:10,099 --> 00:46:11,200
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is already there.

645
00:46:11,520 --> 00:46:12,680
[SPEAKER_00]: The thoughts are already there.

646
00:46:14,101 --> 00:46:18,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you're just discerning them through the basis of act of breathing.

647
00:46:19,285 --> 00:46:20,466
[SPEAKER_00]: And why is that a good thing?

648
00:46:20,786 --> 00:46:25,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Because usually, you discern these things or experience this through the basis of my sense of self.

649
00:46:27,592 --> 00:46:30,014
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, you're doing through the basis that is not your sense of self.

650
00:46:31,255 --> 00:46:36,119
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it makes you understand eventually that your sense of self is completely redundant, whether you choose to do things or not.

651
00:46:38,080 --> 00:46:38,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I said.

652
00:46:39,682 --> 00:46:47,228
[SPEAKER_00]: That feeling that's discerned while doing mindfulness of breathing is feeling among feelings and it develops mindfulness of feelings.

653
00:46:47,288 --> 00:46:48,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Or that's a body among bodies.

654
00:46:49,768 --> 00:46:54,011
[SPEAKER_00]: As in your thoughts of your body position while you're breathing, it's basically where your body is there.

655
00:46:57,553 --> 00:47:04,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing it in that non-appropriated sense, because you're discerning it on the level of non-appropriated breathing.

656
00:47:05,298 --> 00:47:12,422
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you just, my body is seated, I'm seated, I'm seated, that's like you already lost any right principle of mindfulness and you fully appropriated it.

657
00:47:15,384 --> 00:47:17,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're discerning your body being seated.

658
00:47:18,021 --> 00:47:18,962
[SPEAKER_00]: You're discerning you.

659
00:47:19,542 --> 00:47:21,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, while you're breathing, I'm seated.

660
00:47:24,785 --> 00:47:32,611
[SPEAKER_00]: While I'm breathing in deep, slow breaths, I am seated, but I have intention of getting up.

661
00:47:32,631 --> 00:47:33,231
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not doing it.

662
00:47:33,371 --> 00:47:34,192
[SPEAKER_01]: You're discerning it.

663
00:47:36,933 --> 00:47:39,955
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the difference between done rightly and done wrongly.

664
00:47:40,195 --> 00:47:40,776
[SPEAKER_00]: The direction.

665
00:47:41,216 --> 00:47:45,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're doing it, or whether you're actually not doing it, but discerning it, what's being done.

666
00:47:47,502 --> 00:47:55,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're establishing upon your sense of self, as in doing it as a method and a technique, or you're undermining your sense of self, as seeing it, it's done independently of me.

667
00:47:56,869 --> 00:48:02,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Including the presently enduring feeling, the intentions, the thoughts, you discern them, which means they're already there.

668
00:48:02,994 --> 00:48:04,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you want them or not, makes no difference.

669
00:48:06,137 --> 00:48:06,717
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's that.

670
00:48:07,878 --> 00:48:09,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Developing clarity in regard to that.

671
00:48:10,020 --> 00:48:12,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, whether I want it or not, this is a reason.

672
00:48:13,042 --> 00:48:14,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, it's impermanent.

673
00:48:15,024 --> 00:48:16,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything I want or not, is secondary to it.

674
00:48:17,458 --> 00:48:25,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you stop and you lose that gratuitous priority that's assumed to me doing it, me wanting in my sense of self.

675
00:48:30,101 --> 00:48:30,661
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't have to...

676
00:48:30,681 --> 00:48:31,462
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm breathing, I'm breathing.

677
00:48:31,482 --> 00:48:32,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Like now again, I'm still breathing.

678
00:48:33,522 --> 00:48:36,863
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same breath that you had five minutes ago when I asked you the question.

679
00:48:37,593 --> 00:48:43,396
[SPEAKER_00]: The same experience of breathing, many different in and out breaths, but they individually are not your concern.

680
00:48:43,696 --> 00:48:47,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Your concern is overall experience of breathing that you're still mindful as a basis.

681
00:48:48,659 --> 00:48:49,059
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

682
00:48:50,400 --> 00:48:52,601
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through that, you can discern further or not.

683
00:48:53,401 --> 00:48:53,921
[SPEAKER_00]: It's up to you.

684
00:48:54,942 --> 00:49:02,566
[SPEAKER_00]: But not build further or not through some technique or method or step one, step two, but discern what's already there.

685
00:49:03,626 --> 00:49:05,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like, again, ask yourself a question.

686
00:49:05,687 --> 00:49:06,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I breathing

687
00:49:07,141 --> 00:49:14,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Right here, right now, while I'm sitting in this room, you don't even have to say yes, because you already know you are, because you're thinking about it now.

688
00:49:16,507 --> 00:49:22,691
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the characteristics of that experience of breathing?

689
00:49:26,534 --> 00:49:27,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't rush to give the answer.

690
00:49:28,815 --> 00:49:35,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask the question, and allow your mind to endure it, without jumping.

691
00:49:36,493 --> 00:49:37,834
[SPEAKER_00]: The first answer that pleases you.

692
00:49:39,436 --> 00:49:42,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's the characteristic of your overall experience of breathing?

693
00:49:43,559 --> 00:49:44,320
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you describe it?

694
00:49:44,980 --> 00:49:46,382
[SPEAKER_00]: No right answer, no wrong answers.

695
00:49:46,402 --> 00:49:47,242
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you describe it?

696
00:49:48,463 --> 00:49:49,324
[SPEAKER_00]: You let that endure.

697
00:49:53,328 --> 00:49:56,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you be breathing without the notion of the body?

698
00:49:59,233 --> 00:49:59,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Implicit in it?

699
00:50:02,436 --> 00:50:02,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

700
00:50:03,136 --> 00:50:03,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

701
00:50:04,988 --> 00:50:05,889
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an active thing.

702
00:50:06,609 --> 00:50:07,950
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an active thing, sure.

703
00:50:07,970 --> 00:50:08,651
[SPEAKER_00]: It's being done.

704
00:50:09,391 --> 00:50:10,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's done on the basis of the body.

705
00:50:13,995 --> 00:50:15,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is more necessary for the breathing?

706
00:50:16,657 --> 00:50:18,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of the living body and working lungs?

707
00:50:20,500 --> 00:50:23,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you thinking about it?

708
00:50:23,943 --> 00:50:24,503
[SPEAKER_01]: You doing it.

709
00:50:25,124 --> 00:50:26,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is more fundamental?

710
00:50:28,586 --> 00:50:31,088
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the function of lungs in open airways, basically.

711
00:50:31,209 --> 00:50:32,930
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a necessary condition for my breathing.

712
00:50:33,808 --> 00:50:35,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I be alive if I don't breathe?

713
00:50:37,989 --> 00:50:43,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Consequently, can I be alive if my airways get blocked or lungs collapse and stop working?

714
00:50:45,792 --> 00:50:47,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I tell my lungs to not stop working?

715
00:50:48,433 --> 00:50:49,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I command them to behave?

716
00:50:49,694 --> 00:50:50,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I even know how they work?

717
00:50:51,394 --> 00:50:57,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I have some idea, theory from biology and medicine, but... it just happens to me, basically.

718
00:50:59,357 --> 00:51:02,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Dwelling on all these characteristics while you're breathing

719
00:51:03,130 --> 00:51:15,540
[SPEAKER_00]: It means just discerning, just keep hammering it in, basically, that you can't own things even if you want, because even your sense of ownership is because things are given there on their own, independent of you.

720
00:51:18,323 --> 00:51:26,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, oh, by ignoring the fact that things are given independent of you, my lungs, my airways, the breathing, the body, that's how you assume the ownership.

721
00:51:26,710 --> 00:51:29,472
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you either own or you either free yourself from ownership.

722
00:51:31,048 --> 00:51:32,949
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have a view on both.

723
00:51:32,969 --> 00:51:35,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is the ownership, this is the non-ownership, so I choose this.

724
00:51:35,669 --> 00:51:39,251
[SPEAKER_00]: That would require you to step outside your experience, which is inconceivable.

725
00:51:40,211 --> 00:51:59,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just have to keep dwelling on the nature of the very breathing you're doing right here, right now, and you're developing the nature of non-control, the nature of impermanence, the nature of fundamental unsatisfactory experience, because that kind of pleasure we get from things is underlied by the sense of control and joy and accomplishment.

726
00:52:00,505 --> 00:52:10,450
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize that's undermined by the fact that your body is not yours, senses are not yours, no pleasure that comes to your senses can't be yours.

727
00:52:10,950 --> 00:52:12,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean you can't enjoy it.

728
00:52:12,151 --> 00:52:12,551
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

729
00:52:13,992 --> 00:52:15,793
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a big price to pay for enjoying it.

730
00:52:17,654 --> 00:52:19,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Death and deadly suffering.

731
00:52:19,635 --> 00:52:19,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Trap.

732
00:52:20,835 --> 00:52:27,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Through enjoying the pleasures of your eye, you get... your head gets chopped off when that eye disappears, when it fades away.

733
00:52:28,019 --> 00:52:30,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Or even when you're just losing your sight a bit, growing old.

734
00:52:32,901 --> 00:52:33,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Insane goes for any sense.

735
00:52:34,802 --> 00:52:43,988
[SPEAKER_00]: All that, that we talked about, it's based... can be apparent, if you know what questions to ask rightly, no job but answer, and actually look at the discerning aspect of it.

736
00:52:44,628 --> 00:52:47,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Just within you sitting here, breathing in shallowly.

737
00:52:50,571 --> 00:52:50,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling.

738
00:52:51,172 --> 00:52:53,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Talk about... let's think about a feeling while I'm breathing in.

739
00:52:56,395 --> 00:52:57,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm still breathing in.

740
00:52:57,856 --> 00:53:01,778
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I'm saying it because it's for the camera, really, but you don't have to say it yourself, but...

741
00:53:02,796 --> 00:53:04,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask yourself, internally, am I breathing in?

742
00:53:06,757 --> 00:53:09,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Even before you answer it, you already know you're breathing in.

743
00:53:10,879 --> 00:53:14,080
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while you're breathing in, what feeling is enduring there?

744
00:53:18,061 --> 00:53:18,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever it is.

745
00:53:18,701 --> 00:53:19,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant, unpleasant, neutral.

746
00:53:20,242 --> 00:53:20,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

747
00:53:20,762 --> 00:53:21,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't try to define it.

748
00:53:21,802 --> 00:53:24,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Just see which category would fit most accurately.

749
00:53:26,664 --> 00:53:27,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant, I suppose.

750
00:53:29,358 --> 00:53:33,839
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be the characteristics of that pleasant feeling discerned on the basis of me breathing right here, right now?

751
00:53:34,059 --> 00:53:34,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'm still breathing.

752
00:53:37,260 --> 00:53:41,122
[SPEAKER_00]: I can even choose to breathe in long and slow, deep and shallow, whatever.

753
00:53:41,362 --> 00:53:41,882
[SPEAKER_00]: I can choose.

754
00:53:43,362 --> 00:53:45,903
[SPEAKER_00]: The background, how am I feeling while I'm doing all that?

755
00:53:48,384 --> 00:53:48,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, it's pleasant.

756
00:53:49,064 --> 00:53:50,925
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be the characteristics of that pleasant feeling?

757
00:53:51,965 --> 00:53:53,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I discerned it as already being there.

758
00:53:54,686 --> 00:53:58,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Something is already there, means you haven't created it, haven't you?

759
00:53:59,042 --> 00:53:59,722
[SPEAKER_00]: You just found it.

760
00:54:00,563 --> 00:54:05,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would it be a right thing for you, then, to act upon that feeling, to want to prolong it, to want to make... get rid of it?

761
00:54:06,705 --> 00:54:08,126
[SPEAKER_00]: That becomes inconceivable, actually.

762
00:54:08,986 --> 00:54:09,866
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a matter of choice.

763
00:54:10,426 --> 00:54:18,009
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through not being aware of the right nature, correct nature of the feeling, you want to get rid of it, or prolong it, or distract yourself from it.

764
00:54:18,770 --> 00:54:23,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Through being aware of the right nature of that feeling, all these things become inconceivable.

765
00:54:23,572 --> 00:54:24,212
[SPEAKER_00]: It's either-or.

766
00:54:24,852 --> 00:54:26,933
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be aware of the both and then make your choice.

767
00:54:28,630 --> 00:54:31,771
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, oh, I see this, and I see this, and I'm choosing to not do it.

768
00:54:32,231 --> 00:54:33,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you, you're not seeing it rightly.

769
00:54:34,732 --> 00:54:36,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, it's either anuloma or patiloma.

770
00:54:37,593 --> 00:54:44,536
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, it's either with the grain of your senses, of your craving of, of, of the avijjā, or it's against it.

771
00:54:50,398 --> 00:54:52,798
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where restraint is absolutely necessary basis.

772
00:54:53,999 --> 00:54:54,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtue.

773
00:54:55,955 --> 00:55:04,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Because no amount of your discernment, if your body and speech are not restrained and are still going with the grain, you're maintaining the principle of ownership through that grain.

774
00:55:06,940 --> 00:55:14,664
[SPEAKER_01]: It reminds me of that sutta that says, see as though blind, as though you were already blind.

775
00:55:15,564 --> 00:55:16,565
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's what kind of...

776
00:55:17,325 --> 00:55:17,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

777
00:55:17,605 --> 00:55:21,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, basically, see as though you already relinquished your sight.

778
00:55:21,907 --> 00:55:22,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's... that's...

779
00:55:23,822 --> 00:55:24,683
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it means.

780
00:55:24,723 --> 00:55:28,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean, like, pretend you're blind and you're not seeing, because what you're seeing was never the problem.

781
00:55:28,966 --> 00:55:34,610
[SPEAKER_00]: It was you owning your eyes and what it can come through them, that you were affected with everything else that's experienced on account of it.

782
00:55:35,471 --> 00:55:39,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you learn to, like... if you don't forget...

783
00:55:40,474 --> 00:55:46,697
[SPEAKER_00]: The necessary basis for any sight you have are the physical organs of your eyes that you are not in control.

784
00:55:46,897 --> 00:55:49,138
[SPEAKER_00]: They will ultimately diminish and be destroyed.

785
00:55:49,699 --> 00:55:54,241
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot possibly then assume gratuitously have desire towards anything you see.

786
00:55:55,842 --> 00:56:06,527
[SPEAKER_00]: The necessary basis for the desire towards sights is losing the sight, no pun intended, losing the perspective on the nature of the eye that's necessary base for any sights.

787
00:56:08,268 --> 00:56:08,468
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

788
00:56:09,289 --> 00:56:11,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Ultimately, you're going to be blind when you die.

789
00:56:12,372 --> 00:56:13,733
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be deaf when you die.

790
00:56:16,255 --> 00:56:24,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing the ultimate result, death, in regard to your senses, will make you overcome anything that comes to your senses.

791
00:56:25,402 --> 00:56:32,208
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't mean like you're pretending you're not seeing or you're pretending, which is what that wrong view said, that the man who was doing the Buddha said.

792
00:56:32,708 --> 00:56:35,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm acting like I'm blind and deaf, as if

793
00:56:35,654 --> 00:56:37,675
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm ignoring sights and sounds.

794
00:56:37,695 --> 00:56:39,336
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm pretending I'm not seeing and not hearing.

795
00:56:39,356 --> 00:56:41,817
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha said, well, you know better than a blind and a deaf man.

796
00:56:47,781 --> 00:56:48,621
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something different.

797
00:56:49,141 --> 00:56:52,743
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when people blame the sights and sounds and any other senses for their suffering.

798
00:56:53,564 --> 00:57:00,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that suffering is applicable to them, because they appropriated the ownership of the senses, not because of what comes through them.

799
00:57:06,408 --> 00:57:11,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you still breathing while we're discussing this particular approach of senses and eyes and blindness and deafness?

800
00:57:12,553 --> 00:57:12,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

801
00:57:13,194 --> 00:57:13,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Still the same breath.

802
00:57:14,455 --> 00:57:17,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Still the same basis for your mindfulness enduring for like an hour now.

803
00:57:20,059 --> 00:57:21,901
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we've been doing mindfulness of breathing all this time.

804
00:57:22,661 --> 00:57:24,003
[SPEAKER_00]: And discerning the dhamma within it.

805
00:57:25,103 --> 00:57:27,005
[SPEAKER_00]: On the terms of the universal principles.

806
00:57:27,566 --> 00:57:28,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Not on your own terms.

807
00:57:29,367 --> 00:57:30,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Or as accurately as you could do.

808
00:57:31,909 --> 00:57:32,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever way you start.

809
00:57:36,152 --> 00:57:40,274
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, he looks at the signs and characteristics, features of this thing.

810
00:57:41,354 --> 00:57:43,755
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't, like, jump at the answers that his mind provides.

811
00:57:44,195 --> 00:57:44,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I breathing?

812
00:57:44,756 --> 00:57:45,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

813
00:57:45,456 --> 00:57:47,737
[SPEAKER_00]: What would be the characteristics of this breathing?

814
00:57:49,818 --> 00:57:50,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Characteristics.

815
00:57:51,239 --> 00:57:55,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, like, mechanical description, like, my lungs are moving or whatever.

816
00:57:55,300 --> 00:57:56,461
[SPEAKER_00]: That's irrelevant.

817
00:57:57,241 --> 00:57:59,442
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the nature of this act of breathing?

818
00:58:01,583 --> 00:58:03,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I stop it when I want to?

819
00:58:03,284 --> 00:58:03,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Hmm.

820
00:58:04,484 --> 00:58:06,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, only relatively so.

821
00:58:08,485 --> 00:58:10,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I make sure it never goes away?

822
00:58:11,426 --> 00:58:12,126
[SPEAKER_01]: No, not really.

823
00:58:14,846 --> 00:58:17,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have a say in that which is necessary for the act of breathing?

824
00:58:19,127 --> 00:58:21,488
[SPEAKER_00]: The breathing apparatus?

825
00:58:21,508 --> 00:58:22,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the closest.

826
00:58:23,828 --> 00:58:29,829
[SPEAKER_00]: If an accident were to happen, a bacteria or virus to attack these organs, would I still be able to breathe as freely?

827
00:58:30,270 --> 00:58:30,450
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

828
00:58:31,590 --> 00:58:36,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm taking for granted that it will be forever, that it will be in my control, that it will be unobstructed.

829
00:58:37,662 --> 00:58:39,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I taking it for granted?

830
00:58:39,784 --> 00:58:43,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because I'm not thinking sufficiently enough that I cannot take it for granted.

831
00:58:44,409 --> 00:58:44,790
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

832
00:58:45,811 --> 00:58:47,052
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the condition for ignorance?

833
00:58:48,574 --> 00:58:49,735
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ignoring, basically.

834
00:58:50,936 --> 00:58:56,602
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop ignoring, you stop conditioning your ignorance.

835
00:58:56,622 --> 00:58:58,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop ignoring certain universal truths.

836
00:58:58,744 --> 00:58:59,125
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

